# Backend conventions
- Follow the approved Java/Spring versions and existing build system; do not upgrade them implicitly.
- Keep controllers thin, application services explicit, domain logic testable, persistence adapters isolated.
- Prefer Spring JDBC/explicit SQL for high-volume dislocation and complex legacy PL/SQL; JPA only where justified.
- Always bind SQL parameters and set pagination/timeouts. Preserve Oracle transaction and timestamp semantics. Document timezone decisions.
- Structured logs with correlation IDs; never log credentials or raw sensitive payloads.
- External adapters and scheduled jobs must be idempotent, observable, retryable, and independently testable.
- Include unit tests and TEST-Oracle integration tests where available; avoid mocked tests as sole evidence for critical SQL behavior.
